# Keyed blocks

Setting: `ext_keyed`.

A colon pairs a label with one block.

``` example
status: in progress
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>status</dt>
<dd>
<p>in progress</p>
</dd>
</dl>
```

When nothing follows the colon, the block is the one on the following
lines.

``` example
steps:
- one
- two
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>steps</dt>
<dd>
<ul>
<li>
one
</li>
<li>
two
</li>
</ul>
</dd>
</dl>
```

This extension changes how existing djot documents parse: every line it
reads as a key is a paragraph line in djot.

## Where a colon makes a key

A text line is split at its first colon that is

- not escaped;
- not preceded by whitespace;
- followed by a space or by the end of the line (a tab does not count);
- outside every inline construct: not in a code span, a bracket, an
  attribute or an open delimiter.

``` example
see http://example.com: not this colon, but the next
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>see http://example.com</dt>
<dd>
<p>not this colon, but the next</p>
</dd>
</dl>
```

``` example
`a: b` is code
.
<p><code>a: b</code> is code</p>
```

``` example
foo : bar
.
<p>foo : bar</p>
```

The label is one inline element: a run of text, or an emphasis, a link, a
code span.

## The value

What follows the colon on the key line is inline content, never block
syntax.

``` example
foo: - bar
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>foo</dt>
<dd>
<p>- bar</p>
</dd>
</dl>
```

A line is split once.

``` example
foo: bar: baz
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>foo</dt>
<dd>
<p>bar: baz</p>
</dd>
</dl>
```

## One block

A key takes one block, and its scope ends where that block ends. A
paragraph ends at a blank line, a list at a line that is not part of it, a
fenced block at its closing fence.

``` example
foo:
bar

baz
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>foo</dt>
<dd>
<p>bar</p>
</dd>
</dl>
<p>baz</p>
```

Keys nest by line.

``` example
foo:
bar: baz
.
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>foo</dt>
<dd>
<dl class="keyed">
<dt>bar</dt>
<dd>
<p>baz</p>
</dd>
</dl>
</dd>
</dl>
```

## Where a colon is not a key

Lines that djot reads as something other than text are read that way
first: a div fence `:::`, a definition list marker, a reference definition
`[x]: u`, a footnote definition, a heading.

``` example
# foo: bar
.
<section id="foo:-bar">
<h1>foo: bar</h1>
</section>
```

## Output

A description list with one term and the class `keyed`. The tree keeps a
node of its own, with the label and the block.

